nals/honors; I have honors, so not All Simples.  \n\nActually: To win, I must get \u22658 points. Current possible patterns from a complete hand given W7 pung and bamboo/character tiles:  \n\nLet's test completing hand now:  \nIf I meld: Chow1: T6, T7, T8; Chow2: B1, B2, B3; Chow3: B2, B3, B4 not possible with same tiles overlapping. Better plan:  \n\nCurrent groups in hand:  \nA) T6, T7, T8 chow.  \nB) B1, B2, B3 chow.  \nLeftover: T4, B3, B4, F1, J1 \u2192 these 5 tiles, need 1 more meld + pair.  \nPossible meld from T4? Maybe T4, T5, T6 (but T6 used already). Or maybe B3, B4, B5 (don't have B5). Or honors: pair F1 and J1 separately, but need one meld: possible pair F1 and call F1 as final pair if another F1 drawn, or J1 pair.  \n\nActually, with 5 leftover tiles, they could form:  \nOption1: Pair F1, Pair J1, and one tile B4 (needs B5, B6 for chow) \u2013 not enough.  \n\nSo best to discard one honor to keep shape for two possible chows in same suit to get All Chows (2 points) plus maybe Outside Hand (4 points) if all terminals/honors? No, I have simples too.  \n\nLooking at valid actions: Need to discard a tile.  \n\n**Tile discard analysis:**  \n- T4: Isolated in Character suit, no nearby tiles except T6, T7, T8 already used in chow.  \n- T7, T8: Part of chow with T6, so not good to break chow.  \n- B3: I have 2 B3, keep both could make pung or part of two chows (B1B2B3 and B2B3B4). Keep B3.  \n- B1, B2, B4: B1 and B2 needed for B1B2B3 chow. B4 needed for B2B3B4 chow if B2 is kept. So keep B1,B2,B4.  \n- F1, J1: Honors not helping chow formation; they could pair for final pair but only if I get another one. Unlikely given counts in unshown tiles: J2 only 1 left, J1 4 left, F1 4 left.  \nBut F1 is prevailing wind, might be harder for others to discard, but I can draw it myself.  \n\n**Unshown tiles info:**  \n- W3, W4, W7: W7 exhausted (my meld). W3 only 1 left, W4 only 1 left.  \n- T1 (1 left), T2 (2), T3 (2), T4 (3), T5 (4), T6 (3), T7 (3), T8 (3), T9 (2).  \n- B5 only 2 left, B6 3 left, etc.  \n\nGiven my hand structure, to make 3 chows + W7 pung + pair:  \nChow1: T6T7T8 (uses 3 tiles).  \nChow2: B1B2B3 (uses B1,B2,B3 from hand, leaving another B3,B4,T4,F1,J1).  \nThen leftover: B3,B4,T4,F1,J1 \u2192 need one more meld: could be B3B4B5 (need B5) or T4T5T6 (need T5, but T6 used), or F1 pair + J1 pair (but that\u2019s two pairs, not a meld).  \n\nBetter: Keep B3,B4,T4 for meld possibilities, discard one honor.  \n\nWhich honor? F1 or J1? F1 is prevailing wind, others may avoid discarding it, so harder to get another from discard for pung. J1 (Red Dragon) might be easier since only 1 J2 left, maybe they discard J1 sooner. Unshown J1: 4 left, so many in wall. Could self-draw.  \n\nBut if aim for All Types (6 points), need both F1 and J1 in hand at win (wind and dragon present). So keep both F1 and J1? Actually All Types just needs wind tile and dragon tile somewhere in hand, not necessarily in pair or meld. So I could keep one of each.  \n\nBut in final hand, to complete 4 melds+pair, I may have to discard one eventually if can't get a pair. Currently no pair, so must plan to get a pair from F1 or J1 or B3 etc.  \n\nCounting pairs possible:  \nI have B3\u00d72 (potential pair). So pair B3 possible, then F1, J1, B4, T4 are singles. Need meld from T4,B4,F1,J1 \u2192 impossible except maybe B4 with B5B6 or T4 with T5T6.  \n\nSo maybe discard T4 to keep bamboo sequence: B1,B2,B3,B3,B4 \u2192 can make two chows (B1B2B3 and B2B3B4) using B3 twice if overlapping allowed? No, can't use same tile twice except in different melds if I have 2 copies of B3. Actually:  \nB1,B2,B3 chow uses one B3.  \nB2,B3,B4 chow uses other B3 and B2 from first chow? That's illegal, same tile can't be in two melds unless you have two of B2. I have only one B2. So cannot make both chows simultaneously unless I get another B2.  \n\nSo if I keep B1,B2,B3 chow, leftover B3,B4,F1,J1,T4,T6,T7,T8. T6,T7,T8 chow uses those. Then leftover: B3,B4,F1,J1,T4 \u2192 need meld from these 5 tiles \u2192 no.  \n\nGiven the complexity, I should discard a tile that breaks least potential.  \n\n**Best discards:**  \nT4 is most isolated (only one char tile, far from others).  \nF1 and J1 might be kept for All Types but they block making chows.  \n\nPriority: Form two chows (T678 and B123) and then see if remaining tiles can form meld+pair. Remaining after those chows: B3,B4,T4,F1,J1. Need one more chow: could be B3B4B5 (need B5) or T4T5T6 (need T5, T6 used) impossible. So maybe change plan:  \n\nAlternative: Chow T678 (T6,T7,T8), Chow B234 (B2,B3,B4) \u2013 yes I have B2,B3,B4. That uses B2,B3,B4. Leftover: B1, T4, B3, F1, J1. Then can make B1B2B3 chow? But B2 used already. So leftover: B1, B3, T4, F1, J1 \u2192 5 tiles, still need meld+pair.  \n\nActually, both plans stuck.  \n\nMaybe discard J1 or F1 now to keep more suited tiles for flexibility. Since J1 is dragon, may be useful for Two Dragon Pungs if I get another J1 and make pung, but I have only one.  \n\nGiven counts and simplicity, discard an honor to keep suited tiles for chows: F1 or J1. Which? F1 prevailing wind might be riskier to discard (others may win with it). But they already melded W3W4W5 etc., unlikely waiting for F1.  \n\nI'll choose to discard F1 to keep J1 (dragon) for possible dragon pung later if draw another J1.  \n\n**Evaluation:** Current hand is far from winning; aim to improve flexibility. Discarding F1 reduces honor clutter, keeps J1 for dragon possibility, keeps all bamboo and char tiles for chow options.  \n\n**Reason:** F1 is less useful than J1 for potential Dragon Pung scoring, and discarding it opens more chow possibilities by keeping all suited tiles.  \n\n**Answer:** Play F1"}
